\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Equivariant precision and real representation types}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{A symmetry sector need not have a single precision eigenvalue.
Several copies of the same irreducible representation can mix, as the
[half-turn commutant](fdip-003T) already shows. The
[real isotypic projectors](fdip-004V) separate inequivalent types;
within each sector a matrix over the reals, complexes or quaternions
records the remaining freedom.}

\p{The commutant and positive cone determine the size of these matrices.
The Frobenius-Schur indicator then identifies their scalar algebra,
with an explicit passage from complex invariant forms to real modules.
A twisted prism exhibits a complex multiplicity matrix, and the
binary tetrahedral Cayley graph exhibits all three scalar types.
Throughout, a Laplacian block becomes a positive precision block
only after the specified mass shift.}

